<section xml:id="sec-sym-eigenvalues">
  <title>3.1 Symmetric Matrices Have Real Eigenvalues</title>

  <p>First we will show that when <m>A</m> is a real symmetric matrix that for any <m>x \in \mathbb C^n</m> that the quantity <m>Q=\bar x ^T A x</m> is real.</p>

  <p>To show that <m>Q</m> is real, we will make use of the complex conjugate. We use the overbar notation to denote complex conjugate, so if <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are real, then the complex number <m>z = a + i b</m> has complex conjugate <m>\overline{z} = a - ib</m>. Moreover, if <m>\overline{z} = z</m>, then <m>b=0</m> which implies that <m>z</m> must be a real number. We use this idea to show that <m>\overline Q = Q</m>.</p>

  <md>
    <mrow>\overline Q &amp;= \overline{\overline x^T Ax}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;= \overline{\overline x} \, ^T \, \overline{Ax}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;= x^T \, \overline{Ax}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;= x^T A\overline x</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>The last step uses the assumption that <m>A</m> is real, so that <m>\overline A = A</m>. But <m>\overline Q</m> is a number, so <m>\overline Q^T=\overline Q</m> and</p>

  <md>
    <mrow>\overline Q = (\overline Q)^T = (x^T A\overline x )^T = \overline x^T (x^TA)^T =Q</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>Because <m>\overline{Q}=Q</m>, we have shown that <m>Q=\overline x^T Ax</m> is real for any <m>x \in \mathbb C^n</m> and real symmetric <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>.</p>

  <p>Next we use this result to show that when <m>x=v_j</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> that <m>Q</m> is equal to an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.</p>

  <me>Q = \overline x \, ^T Ax = \overline v_j \, ^T A v_j = \overline v_j \, ^T (\lambda_j v_j) = \lambda_j v_j \cdot v_j</me>

  <p>But <m>v_j \cdot v_j</m> is real and <m>Q</m> is real, so <m>\lambda_j</m> must also be real.</p>
</section>
